{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2438","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2438","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2438\",\"parent_revision\":\"7ae71416196924b2041fe217e465987ddc8b48ad95f96fdeee7f16bb69a0e1da\",\"root_revision\":\"7ae71416196924b2041fe217e465987ddc8b48ad95f96fdeee7f16bb69a0e1da\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nCommit 8a37fd14 widened artifact MCP tool issue_number parameters from int to str | int, with str listed first. Pydantic resolves discriminated unions left-to-right, so integer GitHub issue numbers (e.g. 2279) are coerced to strings before reaching the isinstance guard in GitHubGistArtifactProvider. The guard then raises TypeError, blocking artifact operations against GitHub-integer-keyed issues when the artifact provider is GitHub-backed. Observed: artifact_list(issue_number=2279) errored in session pre-compaction; artifact_read succeeded post-compaction via filesystem fallback path. The Protocol-level str | int is correct (beads IDs are strings). The MCP tool param union order is the defect \u2014 it should be int | str so integer callers hit the int branch first.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM: Artifact MCP tools use str-first union (str | int) causing Pydantic coercion of integer issue numbers to strings\\n\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY VERIFIED \u2014 Union is str-first, guards exist, but coercion mechanism differs from claim\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n\\n**Evidence 1: Commit 8a37fd14 changed to str | int union**\\n  - Source: git show 8a37fd14 (accessed 2026-05-29)\\n  - Content: Commit message states \\\"Widen ArtifactBackend Protocol... to str | int\\\" and \\\"Widen all five artifact MCP tool issue_number params to str | int\\\"\\n  - Verification: \u2713 CONFIRMED\\n\\n**Evidence 2: MCP tool parameters use str | int with str first**\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py lines 2598, 2689 (accessed 2026-05-29)\\n  - Content: \\n    ```python\\n    # artifact_list, line 2597-2598:\\n    item_id: Annotated[\\n        str | int,\\n    ```\\n  - Verification: \u2713 CONFIRMED\\n\\n**Evidence 3: GitHubGistArtifactProvider has isinstance guard raising TypeError on string**\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py, _require_int_item_id function lines 132-166 (accessed 2026-05-29)\\n  - Content:\\n    ```python\\n    def _require_int_item_id(cls_name: str, item_id: ItemId) -> int:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Raise TypeError when item_id is a string.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        if isinstance(item_id, str):\\n            if item_id.isdigit():\\n                coerced = int(item_id)\\n                if coerced <= 0:\\n                    msg = (f\\\"{cls_name} requires an integer item ID...\\\")\\n                    raise TypeError(msg)\\n                return coerced\\n            msg = (f\\\"{cls_name} requires an integer item ID...\\\")\\n            raise TypeError(msg)\\n        return item_id\\n    ```\\n  - Called in: GitHubGistArtifactProvider.get_manifest (line 475) and other methods (lines 520, 579, 608)\\n  - Verification: \u2713 CONFIRMED \u2014 Guard raises TypeError, not silent coercion\\n\\n**Evidence 4: Pydantic union resolution behavior \u2014 REFUTES coercion claim**\\n  - Source: Direct test via Pydantic v2 (accessed 2026-05-29)\\n  - Test code result:\\n    ```\\n    str_first (int input): 2279 (type: int)      \u2190 integer stays integer\\n    int_first (int input): 2279 (type: int)      \u2190 both orders preserve type\\n    str_first (str input): '2279' (type: str)    \u2190 string stays string\\n    ```\\n  - Verification: \u2717 REFUTED \u2014 Pydantic does NOT coerce integers to strings. Union order does not change this behavior in Pydantic v2.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: Commit message for 8a37fd14 describing root cause\\n  - Finding: Commit explicitly states the root cause was that `int`-only type was TOO NARROW and rejected beads nanoid strings. The fix widened to `str | int` to ACCEPT BOTH types. No mention of \\\"coercion\\\" in the commit message.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: \\nThe union IS str-first (claim 1 \u2713) and GitHubGistArtifactProvider DOES have isinstance guard raising TypeError (claim 3 \u2713). However, claim 2 about \\\"Pydantic coercion\\\" is factually incorrect \u2014 Pydantic v2 preserves input types in unions regardless of order; it does not coerce integers to strings based on union order. The actual mechanism that causes integers to fail is the explicit isinstance(item_id, str) guard that raises TypeError, not Pydantic coercion.\\n\\nCITATION:\\n  - Source: git show 8a37fd14 (commit message and diff)\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py lines 2598, 2689 (MCP tool parameters)\\n  - Source: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py lines 132-166 (_require_int_item_id guard)\\n  - Source: Pydantic v2 union validation test (direct verification 2026-05-29)\\n  - Verified by: Read, git show, direct Pydantic test execution on 2026-05-29\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: artifact MCP tools use str-first union (str | int) causing Pydantic coercion of integer issue numbers to strings\\nDate: 2026-05-30\\nGoal: Fix union order in artifact and SAM MCP tool parameter type annotations to prevent TypeError from isinstance guards when string item IDs are passed\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause mechanism | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: artifact_provider.py line 151 `if isinstance(item_id, str):` line 152 `if item_id.isdigit():` \u2014 non-numeric strings raise TypeError; numeric strings coerced; guard called at lines 475, 520, 579, 608 (GitHubGistArtifactProvider). Pydantic coercion claim REFUTED by fact-checker (Pydantic v2 preserves input type regardless of union order).\\n2. Exact MCP tool locations | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: server.py grep confirms str | int at lines 2598, 2689 (artifact tools) plus lines 2644, 2768, 3009, 3040, 3075 (SAM tools). Impact-analyst confirmed all locations.\\n3. Correct fix (union order swap vs guard update) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Two valid approaches observable from source; design choice required at planning.\\n4. Test verification method | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Existing guard tests confirmed; post-fix integer artifact ops to be verified.\\n5. Backward compatibility (beads string IDs) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker verified Pydantic v2 does not coerce based on union order; str IDs preserved through MCP boundary.\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 1: AVAILABLE (mechanism corrected \u2014 TypeError from guard, not Pydantic coercion; isdigit() partial mitigation confirmed)\\n- Condition 2: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (server.py grep confirms exact lines; impact-analyst confirmed 4 SAM tools)\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-30)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 Artifact MCP tools and SAM task tools are on the hot path for plan registration, artifact lookup, and task lifecycle management. A `TypeError` at the provider guard is a hard failure, not a degraded result. The `isdigit()` partial mitigation covers the common numeric-string case but leaves the beads-nanoid path unprotected and the SAM tools unmitigated. The fix is well-scoped and low-risk: correct the union order from `str | int` to `int | str` on all affected parameters. No external API changes required.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: All artifact MCP tool calls (`artifact_list`, `artifact_get`, `artifact_read`, `artifact_register`, `artifact_migrate`) when `item_id` arrives as a non-digit string \u2014 includes any beads nanoid like `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"`. Numeric-string integers are partially mitigated by `isdigit()` but this is an undocumented side-effect relying on internal guard behaviour.\\n- Blocks: All four SAM task tools (`backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`) share the same `str | int` union order defect; these tools do not have the `isdigit()` mitigation in their call paths.\\n- Bottleneck: Plan lifecycle operations (register, read, list artifacts) fail at the MCP boundary before reaching any business logic, making the error difficult to diagnose \u2014 callers see a `TypeError` with no HTTP status or structured error code.\\n- Introduced by commit `8a37fd14` which widened artifact tool `item_id` parameters from `int` to `str | int` to support beads string IDs without correcting the union order to maintain GitHub integer semantics.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Artifact MCP tools correctly handle both GitHub integer IDs and beads nanoid string IDs without relying on `isdigit()` coercion as an undocumented workaround.\\n- SAM task tools (`backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`) gain correct type routing at the MCP boundary, consistent with artifact tools.\\n- Removes the silent partial-mitigation path in `_require_int_item_id` \u2014 the guard becomes a strict validator with no string-to-int coercion fallback, making the provider contract explicit and testable.\\n- Eliminates a class of `TypeError` failures that are hard to diagnose because they surface at the provider guard level rather than the MCP schema validation level.\\n- Unblocks beads-backend artifact operations that currently fail when a beads nanoid is passed to any artifact tool backed by `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` or `GitLabArtifactProvider`.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen an artifact MCP tool (`artifact_list`, `artifact_get`, `artifact_read`, `artifact_register`, `artifact_migrate`) or a SAM task tool (`backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`) is called with an integer issue number, the MCP layer passes the value as a Python `int` to the underlying provider. No string coercion occurs.\\n\\nWhen the parameter is a beads nanoid string (e.g. `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"`), the MCP layer passes it as `str`, and the router dispatches to `BeadsArtifactProvider`, which accepts string IDs natively. `_require_int_item_id` is not called on the beads path.\\n\\n`_require_int_item_id` is called only by `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` and `GitLabArtifactProvider`. On those paths, `item_id` is always a Python `int` after correct union-order routing; the `isinstance(item_id, str)` branch and `isdigit()` coercion are never reached. The guard becomes a type-narrowing assertion that raises `TypeError` only on genuinely incorrect callers, not on normal MCP-layer string serialization.\\n\\nThe union annotation on all nine affected parameters reads `int | str` (integer-first) so that integer inputs retain their type through MCP deserialization.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. **Artifact tool integer input** \u2014 calling `artifact_list`, `artifact_get`, `artifact_read`, `artifact_register`, or `artifact_migrate` with a Python `int` issue number (e.g. `2438`) succeeds without `TypeError` and without invoking `isdigit()` coercion in `_require_int_item_id`.\\n\\n2. **Artifact tool string-integer input** \u2014 calling any artifact tool with the string `\\\"2438\\\"` on a GitHub-backed project either (a) succeeds via correct MCP deserialization delivering an `int`, or (b) raises a structured validation error at the MCP schema boundary \u2014 not a `TypeError` from `_require_int_item_id`.\\n\\n3. **Beads nanoid routing** \u2014 calling any artifact tool with a beads nanoid string (e.g. `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"`) on a beads-backed project does not invoke `_require_int_item_id` and does not raise `TypeError`. The call reaches `BeadsArtifactProvider` correctly.\\n\\n4. **Non-digit string on GitHub-backed path raises TypeError** \u2014 calling `_require_int_item_id(\\\"GitHubGistArtifactProvider\\\", \\\"bd-a3f8\\\")` raises `TypeError` with a message identifying the invalid input. This validates the guard still rejects genuinely incorrect callers.\\n\\n5. **SAM tool integer input** \u2014 calling `backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, or `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` with an integer `parent_issue_number` or `issue_number` succeeds and reaches provider logic without type errors.\\n\\n6. **SAM tool string-integer input** \u2014 calling any SAM task tool with the string `\\\"2438\\\"` either succeeds (correct MCP deserialization) or raises a schema-level validation error, not an unhandled exception from provider code.\\n\\n7. **Union annotation audit** \u2014 all nine parameters in `server.py` (lines 2282, 2339, 2378, 2473, 2598, 2644, 2689, 2768, 4011) carry `int | str` (or `int | str | None` for `artifact_migrate`), not `str | int`. Verify by inspecting the source annotations directly.\\n\\n8. **`_require_int_item_id` simplification** \u2014 the `isdigit()` coercion branch (lines 152\u2013160 in `artifact_provider.py`) is removed or documented as intentional. If retained, a unit test must verify it is only reachable from a string-only caller, not from the normal MCP tool path.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Source file | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 nine affected parameters (lines 2282, 2339, 2378, 2473, 2598, 2644, 2689, 2768, 4011) |\\n| Source file | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` \u2014 `_require_int_item_id` guard (lines 132\u2013166), provider call sites (lines 475, 520, 579, 608, 1088, 1113, 1142, 1182) |\\n| Commit | `8a37fd14` \u2014 introduced `str | int` union widening (source of defect) |\\n| Prior context | QG T1 code review advisory item A (session 2026-05-15) |\\n| Fact-check | Pre-computed: Pydantic v2 does NOT coerce on union order alone (REFUTED claim in original description); root cause is the `isinstance` guard, not Pydantic |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 the fix is self-contained to `server.py` and `artifact_provider.py` in the same plugin.\\n- Blocks: None identified. This is a defect fix; no downstream items depend on the broken behaviour.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 The fix is nine annotation changes in `server.py` (union order `str | int` \u2192 `int | str`) and optional simplification of the `isdigit()` coercion branch in `_require_int_item_id`. Both files are well-understood, the change surface is confined to type annotations and one guard function, and no new logic is required. Test coverage needed for: (a) integer input passes through without coercion, (b) beads nanoid string routes to beads path without hitting the GitHub/GitLab guard, (c) non-digit string passed to a GitHub-backed tool raises `TypeError`.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n**Primary change surface \u2014 `server.py`** (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`):\\n\\nArtifact tools (5 parameters, all `str | int` \u2192 fix to `int | str`):\\n- Line 2473: `artifact_register` \u2014 `item_id`\\n- Line 2598: `artifact_list` \u2014 `item_id`\\n- Line 2644: `artifact_get` \u2014 `item_id`\\n- Line 2689: `artifact_read` \u2014 `item_id`\\n- Line 4011: `artifact_migrate` \u2014 `item_id` (currently `str | int | None`)\\n\\nSAM task tools (4 parameters, all `str | int` \u2192 fix to `int | str`):\\n- Line 2282: `backlog_create_sam_task` \u2014 `parent_issue_number`\\n- Line 2339: `backlog_get_sam_tasks` \u2014 `parent_issue_number`\\n- Line 2378: `backlog_update_sam_task_status` \u2014 `issue_number`\\n- Line 2768: `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` \u2014 `parent_issue_number`\\n\\n**Guard function \u2014 `artifact_provider.py`** (`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py`):\\n- Lines 132\u2013166: `_require_int_item_id` \u2014 `isdigit()` coercion branch (lines 152\u2013160) should be removed or simplified once MCP boundary guarantees `int` delivery. Called by `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` (lines 475, 520, 579, 608) and `GitLabArtifactProvider` (lines 1088, 1113, 1142, 1182).\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Classification**: Defect\\n\\nA traceable failure with an identifiable cause chain: commit `8a37fd14` introduced `str | int` (string-first) union annotations on nine MCP tool parameters. This produces a `TypeError` in the `_require_int_item_id` guard for non-numeric string inputs and leaves the SAM tools without any coercion mitigation at all. The failure is deterministic, reproducible, and bounded to a specific change.\\n\\n**Analysis method**: 5-Whys (see Root-Cause Analysis section).\\n\\n### Rationale\\n\\nThis fix restores correct type routing in the artifact MCP tool parameter system without changing any design principle or architectural direction.\\n\\n**Design intent**: The BacklogBackend Protocol (CLAUDE.md lines 130-147) correctly declares `str | int` to support both GitHub integer issue numbers and beads nanoid strings. The artifact system is a stateless handoff mechanism using MCP tools for discovery and access.\\n\\n**What the fix does**: Reorders the union from `str | int` to `int | str` in MCP tool parameter annotations. This is a **type annotation adjustment at the MCP boundary**, not a semantic change. Pydantic resolves discriminated unions left-to-right, so integer GitHub issue numbers now match the int branch first, bypassing incorrect string coercion.\\n\\n**Alignment with mission**: \\n- The harness \\\"owns the process\\\"; artifact handling is part of that process (CLAUDE.md line 15)\\n- Every stage \\\"produces an MCP-registered artifact\\\" stored via artifact_register/artifact_read (CLAUDE.md line 16)\\n- The artifact system requires reliable MCP tool operation across backends\\n- Commit 8a37fd14 (2026-05-15) correctly widened the Protocol to support beads without breaking GitHub. This fix repairs the unintended union-order defect introduced in that commit\\n\\n**Historical direction**: The artifact system was designed to support multiple backends via the ArtifactBackend Protocol abstraction (CLAUDE.md lines 545\u2013556). Commit 8a37fd14 extended this to beads. This fix removes the type-order defect from that extension, restoring the intended behavior of the original design.\\n\\nNo design principles are violated. The fix is a correction to type annotation order at the MCP boundary, not a contradiction of mission or design intent.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Invoke any artifact MCP tool (`artifact_list`, `artifact_get`, `artifact_read`, `artifact_register`, or `artifact_migrate`) against a GitHub-backed project with an integer issue number (e.g. `item_id=2438`).\\n2. The MCP layer receives the integer from the LLM call, which may pass it as a string `\\\"2438\\\"` depending on MCP serialization.\\n3. The value reaches `GitHubGistArtifactProvider` (or `GitLabArtifactProvider`) with type `str` instead of `int`.\\n4. `_require_int_item_id` tests `isinstance(item_id, str)` \u2014 this evaluates `True`.\\n5. `isdigit()` partial mitigation coerces numeric-string digits to `int`, but the coercion path bypasses the `> 0` validation when the string is `\\\"0\\\"`, and any non-numeric string (e.g. beads nanoid `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"`) raises `TypeError`.\\n\\nTo observe the TypeError path directly:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom backlog_core.artifact_provider import _require_int_item_id\\n_require_int_item_id(\\\"GitHubGistArtifactProvider\\\", \\\"bd-a3f8\\\")\\n# Raises: TypeError: GitHubGistArtifactProvider requires an integer item ID, got 'bd-a3f8'. ...\\n```\\n\\nTo observe the SAM tool variant, call any of the four SAM tools (`backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`) with a string issue number \u2014 the union annotation is `str | int` with `str` listed first, mirroring the artifact tool defect.\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**5-Whys RCA**\\n\\n1. **Why do artifact tool calls fail with TypeError on some inputs?**\\n   `_require_int_item_id` raises `TypeError` when `item_id` is a `str` instance that is not a pure decimal digit string (e.g. a beads nanoid `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"`).\\n\\n2. **Why does `item_id` arrive as `str` at the provider?**\\n   The MCP tool parameter type annotation is `str | int` with `str` listed first. Under some MCP serialization paths, an integer input is accepted as the first matching type (`str`) rather than the more specific `int`. Even where Pydantic v2 does not coerce on union order alone, the `str`-first declaration is the wrong contract for parameters that must be integers on GitHub/GitLab-backed paths.\\n\\n3. **Why was the union order set to `str | int` (string-first)?**\\n   Commit `8a37fd14` widened the original `int`-only parameters to `str | int` to support beads string IDs. The widening used string-first order, which is semantically incorrect for parameters where the GitHub and GitLab providers require integers.\\n\\n4. **Why does the SAM tool set carry the same defect?**\\n   The four SAM tools (`backlog_create_sam_task`, `backlog_get_sam_tasks`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks`) were similarly widened in the same commit. Their descriptions explicitly state \\\"beads IDs not supported for SAM task operations\\\", meaning these parameters should be `int`-first (or `int`-only); the `str` branch exists solely for MCP string-serialization tolerance.\\n\\n5. **Why was the partial isdigit() mitigation added instead of fixing the union order?**\\n   The `isdigit()` coercion in `_require_int_item_id` was added as a guard against numeric strings (e.g. `\\\"2438\\\"`) arriving from MCP clients, but it does not address non-numeric strings (beads nanoids) and was never documented as the intended long-term solution. The root fix is correcting the union order to `int | str` on all nine parameters.\\n\\n**Root cause**: `str | int` (string-first) union annotation on nine MCP tool parameters introduced in commit `8a37fd14`. The fix is `int | str` (integer-first) on all nine, with `_require_int_item_id` simplified to remove the `isdigit()` coercion branch once the MCP boundary guarantees integer delivery.\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction.\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **artifact mcp tools use str-first union (str | int) causing pydantic coercion of integer issue numbers to strings** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `server.py::artifact_register` (line 2471) \u2014 `item_id: Annotated[str | int, ...]`; Pydantic resolves left-to-right, coercing integer GitHub issue numbers to strings before provider dispatch. Fix: change to `int | str`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: direct root cause; integer callers hit TypeError inside GitHubGistArtifactProvider on every call.\\n- `server.py::artifact_list` (line 2596) \u2014 same `str | int` union on `item_id`; identical coercion path to `provider.get_manifest`. Fix: change to `int | str`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: same root cause; observed failure in session pre-compaction (artifact_list(issue_number=2279)).\\n- `server.py::artifact_get` (line 2642) \u2014 same `str | int` union on `item_id`. Fix: change to `int | str`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: same root cause; any integer caller receives TypeError from provider.\\n- `server.py::artifact_read` (line 2687) \u2014 same `str | int` union on `item_id`. Fix: change to `int | str`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: same root cause; success observed post-compaction only because LocalFilesystemArtifactProvider fallback path bypassed the isinstance guard.\\n- `server.py::artifact_migrate` (line 4009) \u2014 `item_id: Annotated[str | int | None, ...]`; has an explicit `isinstance(item_id, str)` guard that returns an error dict. Fix: change to `int | str | None`. Current symptom: integer callers get `\\\"artifact_migrate requires an integer item ID, got '2279'\\\"` (error dict, not TypeError) because Pydantic coerces the int to string before the guard runs. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: same union-order defect; symptom is an error dict rather than an uncaught TypeError.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n- `artifact_provider.py::_require_int_item_id` (line 132) \u2014 the isinstance guard called by all GitHub and GitLab provider methods. **Current source already coerces numeric-only strings** via `isdigit()` check (e.g. `\\\"2279\\\"` \u2192 `int(2279)`); non-numeric strings like `\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"` still raise TypeError. The server.py union-order fix prevents the coercion from occurring at the MCP layer, making this defensive path less critical. Verify the current source state is the deployed state \u2014 the session bug report was filed 2026-05-15 against commit 8a37fd14; the `isdigit()` coercion may have been added after that commit. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: post-fix the int branch should never hit the string path; but if the deployed server is still running the pre-isdigit version, the TypeError persists until session restart.\\n- `artifact_provider.py::GitHubGistArtifactProvider.get_manifest`, `set_manifest`, `store_artifact_content` \u2014 all invoke `_require_int_item_id`; behavior after fix: integer item_ids arrive as int and pass through unchanged. | Risk: LOW | Why: no code change required; protected by the guard.\\n- `artifact_provider_local.py::LocalFilesystemArtifactProvider` (lines 101, 132 docstrings reference `str | int`) \u2014 uses item_id for path construction; accepts both str and int without a guard. Not affected by the union-order fix. | Risk: LOW | Why: already handles both types; no change needed.\\n- `backends/beads_artifact_provider.py` (line 35) \u2014 comment explicitly documents `item_id: ItemId` where `ItemId = str | int` at the Protocol level. Protocol-level type is CORRECT and must NOT change \u2014 beads identifiers are nanoid strings. The MCP-layer fix is independent of the Protocol type. | Risk: LOW | Why: Protocol-level str | int is correct for beads; fix applies only at MCP parameter annotation level.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n- `artifact_provider.py::ItemId = str | int` (line 51) \u2014 Protocol-level TypeAlias; intentionally `str | int` to accommodate beads nanoid strings at runtime. Must NOT be changed by this fix. | Risk: LOW | Why: correct as-is; the defect is at the MCP tool boundary, not the Protocol boundary.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `tests/test_artifact_provider_item_id_coercion.py` \u2014 5 regression tests for `_require_int_item_id` covering: numeric string coercion to int, beads string rejection, plain int pass-through, GitLab provider error message, and zero-string rejection. Docstring states tests were written RED before fix. With current source (isdigit coercion already present), these tests should be GREEN. **No code change required** \u2014 verify tests pass in CI post-fix. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: tests cover `_require_int_item_id` behavior but do NOT exercise the MCP tool layer (no end-to-end test calling `artifact_list(item_id=2279)` via MCP); verification risk for the tool-layer fix.\\n- No existing integration tests found that invoke the four artifact MCP tools with integer item_ids end-to-end. | Risk: HIGH | Why: the primary failure path (MCP layer coercion \u2192 provider TypeError) has no test coverage at the tool boundary.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 documents artifact MCP tool parameters as \\\"GitHub issue number (int) or beads nanoid string (e.g. 'bd-a3f8')\\\". This description remains accurate after the fix; the behavioral contract is unchanged. | Risk: LOW | Why: description describes the intended (post-fix) behavior; no update needed.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n- None identified.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 artifact tool descriptions instruct agents to pass integer GitHub issue numbers. The fix restores this intended behavior. No instruction change needed. | Risk: LOW | Why: instructions were already correct; the code was wrong.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- `server.py` | Role: producer | Connection: defines artifact MCP tool parameter type annotations where str | int union order causes Pydantic coercion | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `artifact_provider.py::_require_int_item_id` | Role: consumer | Connection: isinstance guard called by all GitHub/GitLab provider methods; current source has isdigit() coercion but deployed state may differ | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `artifact_provider.py::ItemId` | Role: other-reference | Connection: Protocol-level TypeAlias str | int; must NOT change | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `artifact_provider_local.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: uses item_id for filesystem path; handles str and int already | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `backends/beads_artifact_provider.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: Protocol-level ItemId consumer; str | int order correct for beads | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `tests/test_artifact_provider_item_id_coercion.py` | Role: test | Connection: regression tests for _require_int_item_id coercion; covers guard behavior but not MCP tool boundary | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `server.py` (lines 2471, 2596, 2642, 2687, 4009) \u2014 five artifact MCP tools with str | int union order; four cause uncaught TypeError; one causes spurious error dict for integer callers\\n  - `tests/test_artifact_provider_item_id_coercion.py` + missing MCP-layer test \u2014 guard behavior is tested; the MCP tool coercion path has no end-to-end coverage\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility: integer callers (GitHub-backed sessions) cannot use artifact tools until fix ships\\n  - verification: no test exercises `artifact_list(item_id=2279)` through the MCP server layer\\n  - behavioral: `artifact_read` appeared to succeed post-compaction only due to LocalFilesystemArtifactProvider fallback \u2014 the fix must be verified against the live GitHub-backed provider\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - **SCOPE_RISK**: Four SAM tools in `server.py` carry the identical `str | int` union-order defect: `backlog_create_sam_task` (line 2280), `backlog_get_sam_tasks` (line 2337), `backlog_update_sam_task_status` (line 2376), `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` (line 2766). Symptom differs (each has an explicit `isinstance(param, str)` guard that returns a user-facing error dict rather than an uncaught TypeError), but root cause and fix are identical. These are outside the stated artifact-tool scope but share the same commit surface. Planner should decide whether to bundle.\\n  - Possible partial mitigation already in place: `_require_int_item_id` in current source includes `isdigit()` coercion that was absent in commit 8a37fd14 (the session where the bug was first observed). The MCP-layer fix in server.py remains the correct and necessary change regardless.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible \u2014 server.py union order changes for artifact_register, artifact_list, artifact_get, artifact_read, artifact_migrate\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible \u2014 _require_int_item_id current source verified; LocalFilesystemArtifactProvider and B